<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<pretext>
  
  <docinfo>
    <macros>
      \newcommand{\definiteintegral}[4]{\int_{#1}^{#2}\,#3\,d#4}
      \newcommand{\Z}{\mathbb{Z}}
      \newcommand{\R}{\mathbb{R}}
      \newcommand{\Q}{\mathbb{Q}}
      \newcommand{\N}{\mathbb{N}}
      \newcommand{\empset}{\{\}}
      \DeclareMathOperator{\lcm}{lcm}
      \newcommand{\set}[1]{\{#1\}}
    </macros>
    <latex-image-preamble>
      \usepackage{tikz}
      \usetikzlibrary{shapes.geometric,fit,backgrounds}
    </latex-image-preamble>
  </docinfo>
  
  <slideshow>
    <title>PreTeXt</title>
    <subtitle>Demo Slideshow</subtitle>
    
    <frontmatter>
      <bibinfo>
        <author>
          <personname>Mitch Keller</personname>
          <institution>University of Wisconsin<ndash/>Madison</institution>
        </author>
        <event>MATH 1234</event>
        <date>Summer 2026</date>
      </bibinfo>
      <titlepage>
        <titlepage-items/>
      </titlepage>
    </frontmatter>
    
    
    <section>
      <title>Blank Spots and Big Writing Space</title>
      
      <slide>
        <title>Prime and Composite Numbers</title>
        <p>
          An integer <m>n\gt 1</m> is <term>prime</term> provided that the only positive integers dividing <m>n</m> are <m component="a">1</m><m component="b">\phantom{11}</m> and <m component="a">n</m><m component="b">\phantom{nn}</m>
        </p>
        <p>
          An integer <m>n\gt 1</m> is <term>composite</term> provided that there exists an integer <m>m</m> such that <m component="a">1\lt m\lt n</m><m component="b">\phantom{1\lt m\lt n}</m> and <m component="a">m\mid n</m><m component="b">\phantom{m\mid n}</m>
        </p>      
      </slide>
      <slide>
        <title>Activity</title>
        <p>
          Is the following statement true?
        </p>
        <p>
          <q>If <m>n\gt 1</m> is an integer, then <m>n</m> is either prime or composite but not both.</q>
        </p>
        <p component="a">
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                This is <em>true</em> because negating the definition of prime gives the definition of composite.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                We cannot have free variables in something we plan to prove. Any variable that is not explicitly quantified is assumed to have a universal quantifer applied.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </slide>
      <slide>
        <title>Mid-Slide Whitespace</title>
        <p>
          Suppose we want some space between these paragraphs to write in.
        </p>
        <p component="b">
          <nbsp/>
        </p>
        <p component="a">
          Here's what goes onto the annotated page.
        </p>
        <p>
          This comes next.
        </p>
      </slide>
    </section>
    
    <section>
      <title>SideBySide for Layout</title>
      <slide>
      <title>Cardinality</title>
      <sbsgroup widths="25% 70%">
        <sidebyside>
          <image label="card-inject">
            <shortdescription component="a">An arrow diagram for an injective function</shortdescription>
            <description component="a">
              <p>A set <m>X=\set{a,b,c}</m> and a set <m>Y=\set{5,6,7,8}</m> with a function <m>f\colon X\to Y</m> defined by <m>f(a)=5</m>, <m>f(b)=7</m>, and <m>f(c)=8</m>.
              </p>
            </description>
            <latex-image>
              \begin{tikzpicture}
                  \foreach[count=\i] \lseti/\lsetmi in {X/{$a$,$b$,$c$},Y/{5,6,7,8}} {
                      \begin{scope}[local bounding box=\lseti, x=2cm, y=0.5cm]
                      \foreach[count=\j] \lj in \lsetmi {
                          \node[minimum width=1em] (n-\j-\lseti) at (\i,-\j) {\lj};
                      }
                      \end{scope}
                      \node[ellipse, draw, fit=(\lseti), 
                      label={[name=l-\lseti]above:$\lseti$}] {};
                  }
                  \draw[->] (n-1-X) -- (n-1-Y);
                  \draw[->] (n-2-X) -- (n-3-Y);
                  \draw[->] (n-3-X) -- (n-4-Y);
                  \draw[->] (l-X) -- node[above]{$f$}(l-X.center-|l-Y.west);
                \end{tikzpicture}     
            </latex-image>
          </image>
          <p>
            If <m>f\colon X\to Y</m> is injective, then
            <md component="a">
              |X|\leq |Y|
            </md>
            <md component="b">
              |X|\phantom{\leq} |Y|
            </md>
          </p>
        </sidebyside>
        <sidebyside>
          <image label="card-surject">
            <shortdescription component="a">An arrow diagram for a surjective function</shortdescription>
            <description component="a">
              <p>A set <m>X=\set{a,b,c,d}</m> and a set <m>Y=\set{5,6,7}</m> with a function <m>f\colon X\to Y</m> defined by <m>f(a)=5</m>, <m>f(b)=5</m>, <m>f(c)=7</m>, <m>f(d)=6</m>.
              </p>
            </description>
            <latex-image>
              \begin{tikzpicture} 
                  \foreach[count=\i] \lseti/\lsetmi in {X/{$a$,$b$,$c$,$d$},Y/{5,6,7}} {
                      \begin{scope}[local bounding box=\lseti, x=2cm, y=0.5cm]
                      \foreach[count=\j] \lj in \lsetmi {
                          \node[minimum width=1em] (n-\j-\lseti) at (\i,-\j) {\lj};
                      }
                      \end{scope}
                      \node[ellipse, draw, fit=(\lseti), 
                      label={[name=l-\lseti]above:$\lseti$}] {};
                  }
                  \draw[->] (n-1-X) -- (n-1-Y);
                  \draw[->] (n-2-X) -- (n-1-Y);
                  \draw[->] (n-3-X) -- (n-3-Y);
                  \draw[->] (n-4-X) -- (n-2-Y);
                  \draw[->] (l-X) -- node[above]{$f$}(l-X.center-|l-Y.west);
                \end{tikzpicture}     
            </latex-image>
          </image>
          <p>
            If <m>f\colon X\to Y</m> is surjective, then
            <md component="a">
              |X|\geq |Y|
            </md>
            <md component="b">
              |X|\phantom{\geq} |Y|
            </md>
          </p>
        </sidebyside>
      </sbsgroup>
    </slide>
    <slide>
      <title>Empty Space On the Side</title>
              <sidebyside widths="30% 70%">
          <image>
            <description component="a">
              <p>
                A multigraph with four vertices. There are two edges between vertices 1 and 2. The other edges are <m>\set{1,3}</m>, <m>\set{2,4}</m>, and <m>\set{3,4}</m>.
              </p>
            </description>
            <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="neighbors">
              <diagram dimensions="(250,250)" margins="5">
                <definition>graph={1:[2,2,3],2:[4],3:[4],4:[]}</definition>
                <coordinates bbox="(-1,-1,1,1)">
                  <network graph="graph" scale="0.8"
                    node-fill="#fcf" node-stroke="black"
                    seed="1" labels="yes" 
                    tactile-node-size="40">
                    
                  </network>
                </coordinates>
              </diagram>
            </prefigure>
          </image>
          <p component="b">
            <nbsp/>
          </p>
          <p component="a">
            <ul>
              <li>
                <p >
                  The neighbors of <m>1</m> are <m>2</m> and <m>3</m>
                </p>
                
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>1</m> and <m>4</m> are not adjacent
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\deg(1)=3</m>, <m>\deg(2)=3</m>, <m>\deg(3)=2</m>, <m>\deg(4)=2</m>
                </p>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </p>
        </sidebyside>
    </slide>
  </section>
  <section>
    <title>Pausing</title>
    <slide>
      <title>Pauses</title>
      <p>
        Pauses are not great in slides you intend to <em>annotate</em>, as what you wrote one goes away as you advance through the slides.
      </p>
    </slide>
    <slide>
      <title>Floor and ceiling functions</title>
      <p>
        This demonstrates pausing between list items.
      </p>
      <p>
        <ul pause="yes">
          <li >
            <p>
              The <term>floor function</term> <m>\lfloor\cdot\rfloor\colon \R\to\Z</m> is defined by
              <md>
                \lfloor x\rfloor = \text{the greatest integer }y\leq x
              </md>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              The <term>celing function</term> <m>\lceil\cdot\rceil\colon \R\to\Z</m> is defined by
              <md>
                \lceil x\rceil = \text{the least integer }z\geq x
              </md>
            </p>
          </li>          
        </ul>
      </p>
    </slide>
    <slide>
      <title>Mid-Slide Pause</title>
            
        <p>
          A <term>tree</term> is a connected graph that has no cycles. A <term>forest</term> is a graph with no cycles.
        </p>


        <p pause="yes">
          A <term>rooted tree</term> is a tree with a special vertex designated as the <term>root</term>. Computer scientists are not botanists, so they draw rooted trees with the root at the top. The <term>level</term> of a vertex is its distance from the root. The <term>height</term> of a tree is the largest vertex level.
        </p>
      </slide>
    
    </section>
  </slideshow>
</pretext>